---
description: >
  Two issues found during QA of the shallow-scan description fix: the extractShallowFields fallback path has zero test coverage, and the shallow-mode readFile loads the full file before slicing.
intent: >
  Realign the scan implementation and tests so that the extractShallowFields
  regression path is covered by at least one test that forces parseFrontmatter
  to return null, and so the doc comment accurately describes what the code does.
misalignment:
  - test-gap
  - implementation-drift
---

## Issues

- [x] `src/util/scan/index.ts:121-133` — `readFile` loads the entire file before `buf.slice(0, 500)`. The doc comment claims "reads only the first ~500 bytes to keep startup fast", but every file is fully read into memory then truncated. For large projects with large intent specs, this negates the performance claim entirely.
  Traces to: no named outcome in the spec (utility module, no spec), but the doc comment is the contract and it is false.
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/util/scan/index.test.ts` — `extractShallowFields` is never invoked by any test. All shallow-mode tests supply frontmatter short enough for `parseFrontmatter` to succeed (closing `---` appears well within 500 bytes). The fix was written to handle the case where parseFrontmatter returns null, but that branch has zero test coverage. A regression that deletes `extractShallowFields` entirely would leave all 426 tests green.
  Traces to: the fix description ("When parseFrontmatter returns null, fall back to regex extraction") — the scenario being fixed is not exercised.
  Misalignment: test-gap

## Retro

Both issues would have been caught at the plan stage if a test plan had been written alongside the implementation plan. The test gap in particular is a structural failure: the fix description explicitly names the fallback path as the fix, yet no test was planned that forces parseFrontmatter to return null. Adding a checklist item "write a test where frontmatter YAML block does not close within 500 bytes" to plan.aide would have caught this before implementation landed.

The performance issue would have been caught by a code-review checklist item: "does the implementation match every claim in the doc comment?". The readFile call and the "reads only ~500 bytes" comment are one line apart and contradict each other — a reviewer asking "is this actually lazy?" would have seen it immediately.
